## 4. Edge-cases & how to handle them

| Situation | Why it could trip the detector | Suggested tweak |
|-----------|--------------------------------|-----------------|
| **Custom shield style that hard-codes badge names** (e.g. `"icon-image": "us-interstate"` without `{ref_length}`) | The badge name does not contain `{ref_length}` or `{network}`. | Add a fallback: if `icon-image` matches a known list of badge prefixes (`/^us-(interstate|highway|state)_\d+$/`) **or** if the layer id contains “shield”, treat it as a shield. |
| **Shield layer that uses a separate `icon-image` property for the badge and a separate `text-field` for the number** (exactly the OSM-Bright case)

## TL;DR  

*The current heuristic (`text-anchor` missing **and** `symbol-placement === "point"`) is **not reliable** – it will flag any point-symbol layer that does not explicitly set a text-anchor, which includes the huge majority of place-name / POI layers, and it will miss many real shield layers that use an expression or a zoom-function for `symbol-placement` (or that simply leave `text-anchor` at its default).*

**A better “shield detector” should look at the *signature* of a shield layer, not at the absence of a property.**  
Typical shield layers have **all three** of the following characteristics:

| Feature | Why it matters for shields | Typical value / pattern |
|---------|---------------------------|--------------------------|
| **`icon-image`** | The badge that sits behind the road number. It is built from the network name and the length of the reference (`ref_length`). | An expression that contains `{network}_{ref_length}` or `road_{ref_length}` (or `default_{ref_length}` in OSM Liberty). Often written as a string template: `"{network}_{ref_length}"`, `"road_{ref_length}"`, etc. |
| **`text-field`** | The actual road number that is drawn on top of the badge. | An expression that pulls the `ref` property: `"{ref}"` or `["get","ref"]`. |
| **`icon-text-fit`** (or `icon-text-fit` + `icon-text-fit-padding`) | Makes the badge stretch to accommodate numbers of different length (optional but common). | `"both"` / `"width"` (or `"height"`). If the style uses a *single* stretchable badge, this property will be present; if it uses a pre-drawn badge per length it will be omitted, but the other two clues are still there. |
| **`icon-size` / `icon-allow-overlap`** (optional) | Shields are usually drawn on top of the line and never hidden by other symbols. | `icon-allow-overlap: true` or `icon-overlap: "always"` is common, but not required. |
| **`symbol-placement`** | Shields can appear on points **or** on lines. When they appear on lines the value is an *expression* (object with `stops` or a zoom-function). | Either `"point"` **or** an expression that resolves to `"point"`/`"line"` (e.g. `{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7,"point"],[8,"line"]] }`). |
| **`text-anchor`** | Usually left at the default (`center`) for shields, so the property is often **undefined** – that is *not* a distinguishing feature. | `undefined` (default) – **do not use this as a filter**. |

If a layer satisfies **the first two rows** (icon-image expression that references `ref_length` *and* a text-field that references `ref`), you can safely treat it as a shield. The third row (`icon-text-fit`) is a nice extra signal for “stretchable” shields, but its absence does **not** mean “not a shield”.

---

## Why the current heuristic fails

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // flag as shield
}
```

| Problem | Example that breaks it |
|---------|------------------------|
| **`text-anchor` is undefined for almost every point-symbol layer** (place-names, POIs, city labels, etc.). | `layer.id = "place-city"` – `text-anchor` defaults to `center`, `symbol-placement` is `"point"` → **false positive**. |
| **Real shield layers often use a *zoom-function* or an *expression* for `symbol-placement`** (point at low zoom, line at higher zoom). | OSM Bright’s `highway-shield-us-interstate` uses `{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7,"point"],[8,"line"]] }` → `symbol-placement` is **not** the string `"point"` → **false negative**. |
| **`text-anchor` may be explicitly set to `"center"`** (or any other value) on a shield layer, making the first condition false. | Some custom styles set `text-anchor: "center"` on shields → the layer is missed. |
| **The heuristic does not look at the *icon* at all**, so any point-symbol that happens to have no `text-anchor` will be mis-identified. | A point-symbol for a “tourist-info” icon with no text-anchor → flagged as shield. |

In short: **absence of a property ≠ “this is a shield”.** The presence of *shield-specific* properties is what you need.

